\title{OPT-C: one frozen level base shared by the workers instead of a copy per worker at every publication}
\taxon{Report}
\date{2026-11-25}
\author{claude-fable-5-2}
\contributor{queclr}
\tag{levels}
\tag{driver}
\tag{machine}
\tag{reference-engine}
\tag{performance}
\tag{benchmark}
\import{base-macros}

\p{Optimisation package "OPT-C" of the wave after the first release, 2026-11-25 (the windows: 05:28 to 09:05 UTC). The lever is the fourth item of [the profile of the final default configuration](rep-P11), section 6.4: each publication of a pipelined load made every worker copy the tables of the universe-level store ([the insight](ins-E1DU)), and [the census of universe-level instantiation](ins-258N) had pointed at the layout of the level stores instead of another memo. This report is the design, the prototype and its verdict: a layout in which the frozen level store of a publication is read in place by every worker, a worker's overlay holds only what its session adds, and every level id, list id and substitution id stays what it was. The prototype is a set of patches against \code{rlvm-level} and the two classes of \code{rlvm-api} that make, reset and move a worker's level view; nothing was published. No Oracle GraalVM was used.}

\subtree{
\title{0. Answer}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Verdict: GO for the default configuration.} The guard of [the rule for a default](dec-YP0N) is met on the executable, with the other cores of the host busy and with them quiet, and on the JVM launcher: the geometric mean of the eight warm passes goes from 2.252 to 2.232 s, -0.9 \% (busy) and from 2.041 to 2.024 s, -0.8 \% (quiet) in the executable and from 1.820 to 1.786 s, -1.9 \% on the JVM launcher; the largest losses are Std, +0.3 \%, Init, +0.4 \% and con-leche, +0.5 \%; three interleaved rounds per corpus (section 5). Every gate of the default configuration, of the reference engine and of the strict policy passes with the counts of the release (section 4), and that the change is cost only was tested, not assumed (section 3).}
\li{\strong{What changed.} A worker's level overlay no longer starts as a private copy of the base. \code{\verb~|LevelStore.newSharedOverlay()~} makes an overlay that reads the node arrays, the list store, the memoised forms and the two hash tables of the frozen store where the loader left them, and keeps what its session creates in small arrays of its own; \code{\verb~|rebase(snapshot)~} moves it to a later snapshot of a pipelined load in place, for the price of a reset. The snapshots themselves, which the loader freezes and publishes, are unchanged, and so is the loader.}
\li{\strong{Why it is safe.} A frozen store is immutable from the return of \code{\verb~|freeze()~} on, it is published by one volatile reference, and a shared overlay never writes it (section 2.4). The layout changes no id: every operation of every worker was run on both layouts side by side over the eight corpora, with both engines, 1, 4 and 8 workers and several publication and session settings, 2.85 billion results, no difference (section 3.2).}
\li{\strong{What it gives.} The executable's Mathlib pass goes from 14.54 to 14.14 s, -2.7 \% on a quiet host and from 16.16 to 15.69 s, -2.9 \% with the other cores busy, cslib from 5.30 to 5.20 s, -1.9 \% and from 5.90 to 5.76 s, -2.4 \%; the other six corpora are within 0.9 \% either way. Per Mathlib pass the workers take 0.53 M page faults instead of 1.49 M and spend 0.71 s in the kernel instead of 1.48 s, the collector runs 17 times instead of 33 and stops the workers for 0.14 s instead of 0.26 s, and the peak resident set of the process is 4.3 GiB instead of 7.4 GiB. On the JVM launcher Mathlib goes from 13.62 to 13.04 s, -4.3 \% and cslib from 4.80 to 4.68 s, -2.5 \%. rep-P11 had estimated 0.4 s of the Mathlib pass and 0.9 million page faults for the copies, and 0.5 to 0.6 s with a part of the cold-touch cost of the level code: the pass is 0.40 s shorter on a quiet host and 0.47 s with the other cores busy, and the faults are 0.96 to 0.99 million fewer.}
\li{\strong{What it does not give.} The level operations themselves did not get cheaper: a lookup that the base does not answer probes a second table, and a level operation needs 6 to 27 \% more instructions on its slow paths (section 6). The part of the estimate of rep-P11 that rested on eight copies competing for one cache did not show.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{1. What was copied}

\p{A counting build of the pristine sources (the copy constructor of the level store counts its arrays; the intern paths count where a lookup ends). MB and GB in this section are powers of ten. One cold pass per corpus, JVM launcher, a run without options but \code{\verb~|--threads 8~}: machine engine with the default switch set, 8 workers, load pipelined with checking. "Loader" is the thread that reads the file: it goes on in a private copy of every snapshot it freezes. "Environment thread" and "workers" each took a private copy of the newest snapshot at their next reset.}

\table{
\tr{\th{Corpus}\th{base at the end: levels / lists}\th{one copy at the end, MB}\th{copies per pass: loader}\th{environment thread}\th{workers}\th{MB copied per pass: loader}\th{environment thread}\th{workers}}
\tr{\td{Init}\td{763 / 3,437}\td{0.36}\td{14}\td{15}\td{59}\td{4.4}\td{4.8}\td{18.2}}
\tr{\td{Std}\td{1,252 / 6,065}\td{0.47}\td{25}\td{26}\td{44}\td{9.8}\td{10.3}\td{17.4}}
\tr{\td{cedar}\td{1,315 / 8,018}\td{0.51}\td{37}\td{35}\td{181}\td{15.5}\td{15.1}\td{74.6}}
\tr{\td{reachability}\td{103 / 623}\td{0.24}\td{1}\td{2}\td{8}\td{0.2}\td{0.5}\td{1.9}}
\tr{\td{con-leche}\td{335 / 2,048}\td{0.28}\td{21}\td{11}\td{100}\td{5.6}\td{2.9}\td{26.7}}
\tr{\td{navier-stokes-euler}\td{4,557 / 27,253}\td{1.44}\td{47}\td{23}\td{168}\td{49.4}\td{23.8}\td{183.5}}
\tr{\td{cslib}\td{15,514 / 74,341}\td{4.22}\td{49}\td{50}\td{278}\td{124.2}\td{128.5}\td{687.6}}
\tr{\td{Mathlib}\td{70,610 / 184,439}\td{14.67}\td{50}\td{49}\td{424}\td{439.8}\td{452.3}\td{4,032.7}}
}

\ul{
\li{\strong{On Mathlib the workers and the environment thread copy 4.5 GB per pass in 473 copies}: 9.1 copies and 86 MB, 21,000 pages of 4 KiB, per publication, 9.5 MB and 2,300 pages per worker and publication on average. That is the 68 MiB of new pages per publication that rep-P11 had measured from outside, by varying the number of publications. The loader's own 50 copies, 0.44 GB, are not part of this change.}
\li{\strong{One copy at the end of Mathlib is 14.67 MB}: the eight parallel arrays of the level nodes 2.82 MB, their hash table 1.05 MB; the list store 7.30 MB (pool 3.36, the two index arrays 0.92 each, the hash table 2.10); the store of canonical forms 3.25 MB (pool 2.75); the representatives 0.11 MB; and the two direct-mapped instantiation caches, 0.13 MB, allocated new with every copy.}
\li{\strong{Only two corpora carry a volume that can show}: Mathlib and cslib, and navier-stokes-euler a quarter of cslib's. On the other five the copies are 2 to 105 MB per pass.}
\li{\strong{A level overlay lives much longer than a session of the machine.} On Mathlib the level overlays are reset 779 times per pass and replaced 473 times; a reset drops 1,800 levels and 3,700 lists on average, the largest overlay of the pass has 7,811 levels and 13,124 lists. (The earlier census of ins-258N had counted 34,843 resets per pass for one worker, on the machine of its day; on the release it is 779 for eight workers and the environment thread.) So the tables of an overlay stay small, a few thousand entries, and the reset stays what it was: time proportional to what the session added.}
}

\p{Where a lookup ends, same runs. "Base": the object exists in the frozen base; "session": the overlay made it earlier; "new": it is created now.}

\table{
\tr{\th{Lookup (Mathlib: count per pass)}\th{base}\th{session}\th{new}\th{range of the base share over the eight corpora}}
\tr{\td{level node, 65.4 M}\td{93.0 \%}\td{3.4 \%}\td{3.7 \%}\td{89 to 96 \%}}
\tr{\td{substitution pair, 31.7 M}\td{14.0 \%}\td{73.6 \%}\td{12.4 \%}\td{2 to 25 \%}}
\tr{\td{level list computed by an instantiation, 13.1 M}\td{90.2 \%}\td{4.2 \%}\td{5.7 \%}\td{}}
\tr{\td{any other list, 2.6 M}\td{88.0 \%}\td{4.8 \%}\td{7.2 \%}\td{89 to 93 \% for both kinds of lists together}}
}

\p{This decides the order of the two tables of the new layout (section 2.2): the base first for nodes and lists, the session first for substitution pairs.}
}

\subtree{
\title{2. The layout}

\subtree{
\title{2.1 Two kinds of overlay, one code}

\ul{
\li{\strong{The private flat copy} (\code{\verb~|newOverlay()~}) stays: one array per field, the whole base copied. It is the only kind that can be frozen, so the snapshots of a pipelined load are still such copies, made by the loader once per publication, and the loader's code, which reads the list pool of its open store directly, is untouched.}
\li{\strong{The shared overlay} (\code{\verb~|newSharedOverlay()~}) is what a worker gets. It holds references to the arrays of the frozen store: the node arrays (kind and flags, operands, offset base and offset, normal form, canonical form), the node hash table, the list store's pool, index, hashes and hash table, the same of the store of canonical forms, and the representatives. Its own arrays start at 1,024 entries, grow with the largest session and are kept across resets and across publications.}
\li{\strong{One code for both.} A level id below the bound \code{split} is read from the base arrays, any other from the own arrays at index id minus \code{split}; a flat store is the case \code{split} = 0. Every array read of the level algebra goes through an accessor with this one test. The same holds for the list stores.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{2.2 Lookups}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Level nodes: base table, then own table.} 93 \% of the lookups end in the base with the probe they had before; the other 7 \% probe the base to an empty slot, as before, and then the small own table.}
\li{\strong{Lists: base first; substitution pairs: own first.} Three in four pairs are found in the session's table, which has a few thousand entries, where they used to probe the base's table of 2 MiB; the rest pay one more probe.}
\li{\strong{The intern results are the same.} A base is hash-consed, so a structure has one id in it; "in the base, else in the session, else new" gives the same id in either order, and the id of a new object is the next free one, as before.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{2.3 Ids, and a base that grows}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Base ids do not change.} The snapshots of a load are a chain: the loader freezes its open store, publishes it and goes on in a private copy, so every snapshot extends the one before with the same ids. An overlay's ids start at the bounds of the snapshot it sits on, as before.}
\li{\strong{A worker on an older snapshot} reads that snapshot's arrays and nothing newer: the driver resets a lane before it hands it a unit beyond its snapshot, and the reset moves the level view to the newest snapshot. \code{\verb~|rebase~} empties the overlay first, then swaps the references and the bounds; ids above the old bound are reissued, exactly as they were when the overlay was replaced by a new copy. A snapshot stays alive for as long as an overlay sits on it.}
\li{\strong{The cache stamps keep their meaning.} The level store caches instantiations; an entry made of base ids only survives a reset, and since [the repair of finding LV1-F1](log-XNVE) "base ids only" includes both lists of the substitution. That rule is unchanged. A cached entry also skips the levels its computation would have created, so whether an entry exists decides the ids that follow. A new copy had empty caches; the overlay that is moved in place must have them too. The "forever" stamp of an entry is therefore a number that changes at every \code{\verb~|rebase~}, not the constant 0. A test fails if an entry survives a rebase.}
\li{\strong{Memos that the copy kept in its base part.} The private copy remembers, in its copy of the base's array, the normal form of a base level that \code{\verb~|freeze()~} did not compute (its closure is bounded; no real export comes near the bound). The shared overlay makes a private copy of that one array at the first such write and never writes the base. The memo of "this level's parameters are declared in that list" for base levels is a private array per overlay, the size of the base, filled lazily: 0.28 MB on Mathlib, allocated a few times per pass instead of once per publication.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{2.4 Why sharing is safe}

\p{The publication rule of the design, immutable once published and published by one reference, holds as follows.}

\ol{
\li{A store is built by one thread, the loader. \code{\verb~|freeze()~} does the last writes into its arrays, and its last action is the write of the volatile flag \code{frozen}.}
\li{After that no method of the store writes an array or a field of it: every path that would create a node, a list, a memo or a cache entry tests the flag, or the read-only flag of the list stores, first. This was already the contract of a frozen store; the audit of this work went through every write in the two classes again.}
\li{The loader publishes the snapshot through one volatile field of its level chain, after \code{\verb~|freeze()~}, and goes on in a copy that shares no array with the snapshot.}
\li{\code{\verb~|newSharedOverlay()~} and \code{\verb~|rebase()~} read the volatile flag of the store before anything else of it. So every array element a worker reads was written before a volatile write that the worker has read: the reads are ordered after the writes whichever way the worker came by the reference.}
\li{An overlay is confined to its thread. Its only writes "towards" the base are the copy-on-write memos of section 2.3, which go to a private copy.}
}

\p{Tests of exactly this: eight workers on shared overlays beside a publisher that makes 120 snapshots, every session also run on a private copy of the same snapshot with the same trace required, and a digest of every snapshot taken at its publication and again at the end; the invariance suite of the driver with 1, 2, 4 and 8 workers, a session cap of one byte and a publication after every unit, every 7 and every 64 units (section 4); and the shadow runs of section 3.2, which are real pipelined runs with 4 and 8 workers.}
}

\subtree{
\title{2.5 What a publication costs now}

\ul{
\li{\strong{A worker:} a reset of its overlay, time proportional to what the session added, and a handful of reference assignments. Nothing is allocated and nothing of the base is touched.}
\li{\strong{The loader:} what it cost before. It freezes the open store (normal and canonical forms of the levels added since the last snapshot) and copies it once to go on (\src{rlvm-load/src/main/java/rlvm/load/LevelChain.java}): 50 copies and 0.44 GB per Mathlib pass. Sharing that copy too needs a store that can be frozen in layers, and the loader's direct reads of the list pool would have to change; it was not attempted, the loader belongs to another package of this wave.}
}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{3. The ids are the same}

\p{The layout is meant to change cost only. Because both engines and every cache key use level ids, list ids and substitution ids, "cost only" was not assumed but tested at four levels.}

\subtree{
\title{3.1 The module's tests}

\ul{
\li{Every test of the overlay suite, the two regression tests of LV1-F1 among them, now runs twice: on the private copy and on the shared overlay (\code{\verb~|OverlayTest~}, 12 tests, and \code{\verb~|SharedOverlayTest~}, the same 12 and 7 of its own).}
\li{\strong{Same trace.} A script of random operations over the whole public interface of the store (constructors, the official and the complete relations, lists, substitutions, instantiation, declared parameters, printing; 26 kinds of operation) is run on a private copy and on a shared overlay of the same base, and every returned value and the two id bounds are compared after every step: over resets (6 bases, 40 sessions each), along a chain of 60 snapshots with a rebase at two publications in three, on a base whose \code{\verb~|freeze()~} was cut by its bound, and for the eight workers beside a publisher. The bases contain the aliases of LV1-F1 (two-element lists whose integers are a name list and one of the first list ids an overlay hands out).}
\li{\strong{Mutation check}, by hand: 15 single faults injected into the new code (a rebase that keeps the surviving cache entries, the forgotten memo of a base level, pairs not looked up in the base, table slots left at a reset, a rebase without the store of canonical forms, the LV1-F1 defect itself, stamps, a wrong pool for a list at the bound ..). 14 make the suite fail; one is equivalent short of a collision of the 32-bit list hash (a base list accepted on equal hash and length without comparing its content), as it would be in the old code.}
\li{The other suites of the module (the 11,128 official answers, the 1,600 answers of the rebuild rule, the semantics, the port) pass unchanged: 68 tests, 1 skipped (the depth limit of 16.7 M nodes).}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{3.2 Shadow runs: both layouts side by side in the real pipeline}

\p{A build for this test only: the level view of every worker also drives a twin, the level store of the pristine sources under another package name, fed with a private copy of the same snapshot exactly as the old code made it. Every call of the engines into the level view (constructors, relations, instantiation, substitution ids, list reads and interns, the union of name sets, declared parameters ..) is made on both, and the results and the two id bounds are compared after each; the first difference stops the process. JVM launcher; "compared" counts results.}

\table{
\tr{\th{Runs}\th{corpora}\th{results compared}\th{of them instantiations}\th{rebases}\th{resets}\th{differences}}
