\subtree{
\title{3.4 The machine's gate}

\p{The gate of package M8 (\code{\verb~|report.sh gate --switches default --session-mib 1~}), once from the pristine sources and once with the changed \code{rlvm-level} and \code{rlvm-api}: both PASS WITH DECLINES, the known decline of one unit of Std. Of the 505 cells of the two reports (differential against the reference engine per declaration, tier equivalence, convention equivalence with every record word compared, the debug audits, the real driver on the 230 tests and four corpora) 500 are identical, ticks and frames included; the five that differ are seconds.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{4. Gates}

\p{All on the private distribution of the candidate (everything compiled from a private copy of the tree; jars sha256 \code{6368fa1335cf}), through its own launchers.}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Unit suites}, every module against the changed \code{rlvm-level} and \code{rlvm-api}: \code{rlvm-level} 68 tests (1 skipped), \code{rlvm-api} 32, \code{rlvm-store} 71, \code{rlvm-load} 81, \code{rlvm-nat} 54, \code{rlvm-inductive} 91, \code{rlvm-admit} 89, \code{rlvm-ref} 148, \code{rlvm-driver} 107, \code{rlvm-main} 64, \code{rlvm-cli} 49, the fast group of \code{rlvm-machine} 420: no failure. \code{\verb~|SHostileSlowTest~}: 56 switch sets times 1,196 hostile exports, nothing unexpected under any set.}
\li{\strong{The gate of the default configuration through the executable} (\code{\verb~|scripts/gate.sh --defaults --corpus all --hostile --native~}): 230 of 230 arena tests; 17,775 mutants with SOUNDNESS 0 and COMPLETENESS 0 (agree 15,739, declined 2,034, 2 timeouts, see below); the self-test of the differential path caught; 1,196 hostile exports: agree 1,124, 68 tolerated accepts, 0 wrong accept, 0 wrong reject, 4 declined. PASS.}
\li{\strong{The same through the jars}: 230 of 230; 17,775 mutants with SOUNDNESS 0 and COMPLETENESS 0 (agree 15,739, declined 2,034, the same 2 timeouts); self-test caught; hostile: agree 1,124, 68 tolerated, 0 wrong accept, 0 wrong reject, 4 declined. PASS.}
\li{\strong{The two timeouts} are the mutants of \code{bugs/proj-of-subst-prop} and \code{bugs/rec-of-subst-prop} that change a literal to 2^64 - 1 and 2^64: one rejected declaration each that keeps the machine busy for 20 s on a quiet core. The gates ran on the efficiency cores of a host with a load average of 30 to 45, where the limit of 60 s per mutant was passed by both distributions: taken alone at that time, the pristine executable needs 33 s and the candidate's 42 to 45 s (on quiet cores 20.5 and 21.5 s, section 7); through the jars the pristine distribution needs 59 to 105 s and the candidate 56 to 82 s. Both reject, which is the official verdict. A second run of the corpus \code{bugs} on both launchers, with the other gates of this package finished: agree 2,590, declined 102, no timeout, on the executable and through the jars. With it both gates have the counts of the release: agree 15,741, declined 2,034.}
\li{\strong{The reference engine by name} (\code{\verb~|scripts/gate.sh --corpus all --hostile~}, which runs \code{\verb~|-X engine=reference~}, batch load, one worker, through the jars): 230 of 230; 17,775 mutants with SOUNDNESS 0 and COMPLETENESS 0 (agree 15,741, declined 2,034, no timeout: the counts of the release); self-test caught; hostile: agree 1,182, 6 tolerated accepts, 0 wrong accept, 0 wrong reject, 8 declined. PASS.}
\li{\strong{The strict policy} (the same with \code{\verb~|-X policy=strict~}, the mutants of the corpus \code{bugs}): 230 of 230; 2,692 mutants with SOUNDNESS 0 and COMPLETENESS 0 (agree 2,579, declined 113, no timeout); hostile: agree 1,177, no tolerated accept, 0 wrong accept, 0 wrong reject, 19 declined. PASS.}
\li{\strong{The eight corpora} accepted with 8 and with 4 workers through the executable and through the JVM launcher: 32 of 32 runs.}
\li{\strong{The invariance suite of the driver} (every file under 9 schedules: batch with 1, 2, 4 and 8 workers, a session cap of one byte, pipelined with a publication after every unit, every 7 and every 64 units, with 1, 4 and 8 workers). With the machine as primary engine: the 231 published tests, the four large exports (init-prelude, Init, Std, cedar), 12,260 mutants of the tutorial tests and 2,692 of the corpus \code{bugs}: 0 wrong, 0 differences between schedules, 0 declines that only a pipelined run has; 478 mutants declined alike in every schedule. With the reference engine: the 231 published tests and the 12,260 tutorial mutants: 0 wrong, 0 differences, 413 declined alike.}
\li{\strong{The machine's gate} and the counters of section 3: identical.}
}

\p{The gated distribution was built before a last change of a comment; the byte code of every class of the two changed jars is the same as in a build of the final sources (the level store's class differs in its line number table only).}
}

\subtree{
\title{5. The guard}

\p{[The rule for a default](dec-YP0N): the geometric mean of the warm passes of the eight corpora goes down and no corpus is more than 3 \% slower, over at least three interleaved rounds. Warm pass that reloads the file, 8 workers, load pipelined with checking, a run without options but \code{\verb~|--threads 8 -X passes=N~}; the 8 measurement cores of the aarch64 development host; every corpus in one quiet-core window with base and candidate alternating round by round (A B, B A, A B). A cell is the mean over the rounds of the median later pass of one process, in brackets the smallest and the largest of the rounds; on a pass shorter than 0.5 s the mean of the later passes. Base and candidate are two distributions built the same way from two copies of the tree that differ by the patches only. \strong{The other cores of the host were busy during these windows} (load average 23 to 45: this package's own gates and the work of the other packages), which is the condition of the benchmark tables and not that of rep-P11: the absolute times are 10 to 13 \% above rep-P11's. Section 5.4 repeats the table of the executable with the other cores quiet.}

