\ul{
\li{\strong{The guard is met}: the geometric mean goes from 2.252 to 2.232 s, -0.9 \%, and the largest loss is Std, +0.3 \%, inside the spread of its own rounds.}
\li{\strong{The workers' page faults fall by 0.96 million per Mathlib pass}, from 1.49 M to 0.53 M, and by 0.20 M on cslib: the copies. rep-P11 had put 0.9 M of the workers' 1.5 M on them. Kernel time of the workers 1.48 to 0.71 s on Mathlib, 0.57 to 0.34 s on cslib. The loader thread is unchanged, 0.85 M faults and 12.3 s of CPU on Mathlib: it still copies once per publication.}
\li{\strong{Half the collections}: 33 to 17 per Mathlib pass and 12 to 8 on cslib, pauses 0.26 to 0.14 s and 0.10 to 0.05 s. The copies were 4.5 GB of the garbage of a pass.}
\li{\strong{Peak resident set}: 7.4 to 4.3 GiB on Mathlib (7,549 to 4,405 MiB), 2.4 to 2.0 GiB on cslib, 9 to 14 \% less on Init, Std, cedar and navier-stokes-euler, equal on reachability and con-leche.}
\li{\strong{The workers' CPU outside the kernel} falls by 1.5 \% on Mathlib (106.4 to 104.7 s) and by 0.9 \% on cslib (36.9 to 36.5 s); on the other six by 0.0 to 0.9 \%. On Mathlib the workers need 2.4 s less CPU in all, which is 0.30 s of the pass by the rule of rep-P11 (a CPU second is an eighth of a second), and the pauses are 0.12 s shorter: 0.42 s of the measured 0.47 s.}
\li{\strong{A first pair of Mathlib processes}, taken an hour earlier with less load on the other cores (one process each, later passes): 14.56 and 15.03 s before, 14.32 and 14.54 s after; workers' page faults 1.51 M and 0.54 M, kernel time 1.54 and 0.65 s, peak resident set 7,556 and 4,377 MiB.}
}
}

