\li{\strong{Mathlib} loses 0.58 s, more than in the executable, and with more spread: the rounds of the base are 13.15, 13.97 and 13.73 s, those of the candidate 13.01, 13.42 and 12.68 s. The JVM takes no page faults for the copies (its young generation is touched once); it saves the copying and half of the pauses, 0.32 to 0.17 s per pass. Workers' CPU 88.7 to 84.9 s, where the worker CPU of a JVM pass repeats within 2.5 \% (rep-P11). Peak resident set 6,218 to 5,147 MiB.}
\li{\strong{Std}, -3.4 \%, is more than its copies can explain (28 MB per pass): the candidate has no collection in its nine later passes where the base has one or two of 20 ms, and the rest is the spread of a pass of one second (the medians of the rounds: 1.015, 1.057 and 1.057 s against 1.029, 0.995 and 0.999 s).}
\li{The workers' CPU is equal or lower on every corpus but con-leche (7.19 to 7.27 s, inside the spread of the rounds: 7.04 to 7.36 s and 7.12 to 7.48 s).}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{5.3 Smaller pieces again? The hand-off interval on a shared base}

\p{The driver publishes a large file in larger pieces, the hand-off interval times the size of the input in GiB and at most 4 times, because every publication cost every worker a copy ([the insight](ins-E1DU)): Mathlib in 52 pieces instead of 205. rep-P11 measured 15.04 to 15.17 s with 205 publications against 14.59 to 14.61 s with 52, and expected that a shared base would let the file be published in small pieces again. An experiment that changes no code: the executable with \code{\verb~|-X publish-scale=1~}, which takes the scaling away, candidate and base, against the candidate with the default interval. One window per corpus, the three variants alternating, two rounds; a cell is the mean of the two median later passes, in brackets the two.}

\table{
\tr{\th{Variant}\th{pass, s}\th{workers: CPU s}\th{workers: kernel s}\th{workers: page faults}\th{loader thread: CPU s}\th{loader thread: kernel s}\th{pauses per pass, ms}\th{peak RSS, MiB}}
\tr{\td{Mathlib: candidate, the default interval}\td{15.05 (14.72, 15.37)}\td{100.9}\td{0.71}\td{550,141}\td{11.9}\td{1.10}\td{141}\td{4,491}}
\tr{\td{Mathlib: candidate, \code{\verb~|-X publish-scale=1~}}\td{15.00 (14.54, 15.46)}\td{101.1}\td{0.68}\td{521,880}\td{12.3}\td{1.43}\td{200}\td{4,994}}
\tr{\td{Mathlib: base, \code{\verb~|-X publish-scale=1~}}\td{15.99 (15.84, 16.15)}\td{104.3}\td{2.43}\td{2,515,484}\td{12.4}\td{1.46}\td{552}\td{11,500}}
\tr{\td{cslib: candidate, the default interval}\td{5.22 (5.21, 5.22)}\td{33.7}\td{0.34}\td{291,999}\td{4.7}\td{0.44}\td{47}\td{1,991}}
\tr{\td{cslib: candidate, \code{\verb~|-X publish-scale=1~}}\td{5.26 (5.19, 5.33)}\td{33.8}\td{0.34}\td{293,478}\td{4.8}\td{0.48}\td{59}\td{2,147}}
\tr{\td{cslib: base, \code{\verb~|-X publish-scale=1~}}\td{5.38 (5.37, 5.38)}\td{34.1}\td{0.64}\td{567,057}\td{4.7}\td{0.47}\td{140}\td{2,637}}
}

\ul{
\li{\strong{With the shared base the small pieces cost the workers nothing}: the candidate needs 15.00 s for Mathlib in 205 pieces and 15.05 s in 52, the same workers' CPU, page faults and kernel time; cslib 5.26 and 5.22 s. The base with 205 pieces needs 15.99 s and 11,500 MiB, with 2.5 million page faults in the workers: at that interval the shared base is worth 1.0 s of the Mathlib pass and 6.4 GiB.}
\li{\strong{But they are no gain either.} The workers are not waiting for the loader on these corpora, so finer pieces feed them no better; and the loader, which still copies its store at every publication, pays for them: on Mathlib 1.43 s of kernel time instead of 1.10 s, pauses of 200 ms per pass instead of 141 ms, 4,994 MiB resident instead of 4,491.}
\li{\strong{So the interval stays.} The reason it was scaled is gone, and nothing in these two windows asks for another value. Two rounds per variant; inside the Mathlib window the second round was 0.3 to 0.9 s faster than the first for every variant, the other cores had got quieter.}
}
}

