\li{\strong{The counts do not depend on the other cores}: page faults of the workers 1.52 M to 0.53 M per Mathlib pass, their kernel time 1.52 to 0.66 s, pauses 0.27 to 0.14 s, peak resident set 7,725 to 4,463 MiB. What depends on them is the price of the same copies: 0.47 s of the Mathlib pass with the other cores busy, 0.40 s with them quiet; 0.14 and 0.10 s on cslib.}
\li{\strong{The six other corpora} are within 0.9 \% either way in both conditions; navier-stokes-euler, whose copies are a quarter of cslib's, shows nothing (+0.1 \% here, -0.3 \% busy). Its peak resident set of 1,924 MiB for the candidate is one round of three; the other two are 1,432 and 1,455 MiB.}
}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{6. What a lookup costs on a shared base}

\p{Instructions per operation, the module's benchmark (\src{bench/modules/rlvm-level/run_bench.py}, now with \code{\verb~|--overlay shared|flat~}): two JVM runs per scenario in the arena container, the difference divided by the difference of the iteration counts; GraalVM CE 25 JIT, synthetic small levels. "Flat" is the private copy with the new code, "shared" the overlay of a worker. Repeatability 0.1 to 1.5 instructions.}

\table{
\tr{\th{Operation}\th{flat}\th{shared}\th{difference}}
\tr{\td{the loop alone}\td{15.1}\td{15.1}\td{0.0}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|isEquiv(l, l)~}, identical ids}\td{14.7}\td{14.9}\td{+0.2}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|isEquiv~}, canonical forms memoised}\td{31.5}\td{35.5}\td{+4.0}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|isEquivOfficial~}, normal forms memoised}\td{29.5}\td{36.1}\td{+6.5}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|normalize~} / \code{\verb~|canonId~}, memo hit}\td{21.0 / 20.9}\td{23.5 / 24.0}\td{+2.5 / +3.2}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|instantiate~}, identity / closed level}\td{15.0 / 21.5}\td{15.0 / 23.6}\td{0.0 / +2.1}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|instantiate~}, cache hit}\td{42.0}\td{51.9}\td{+10.0}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|instantiateList~}, cache hit}\td{36.5}\td{36.5}\td{0.0}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|undeclaredParam~}, memo hit}\td{28.6}\td{28.5}\td{-0.1}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|mkMax~} / \code{\verb~|mkIMax~}, result exists in the base}\td{121.6 / 126.4}\td{135.2 / 160.4}\td{+13.5 / +33.9}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|isGeq~} on two stored canonical forms}\td{439.3}\td{442.0}\td{+2.7}}
\tr{\td{build a new small level (1 or 2 new nodes), reset every 16}\td{414.4}\td{451.1}\td{+36.6}}
\tr{\td{intern a new list of 3 levels and a substitution, reset every 16}\td{428.7}\td{510.7}\td{+82.0}}
\tr{\td{the same and two instantiations that miss the cache}\td{1,641.7}\td{2,023.9}\td{+382.2}}
\tr{\td{build a new small level and \code{\verb~|normalize~} it}\td{1,236.1}\td{1,415.7}\td{+179.6}}
\tr{\td{build a new small level and \code{\verb~|canonId~} of it}\td{2,315.4}\td{2,446.2}\td{+130.8}}
}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Per array read about 4 instructions}: the test against \code{split} and the second array. The hits of the two caches, which are the common case behind the machine's own cache, cost 0 and 10 instructions more.}
\li{\strong{The slow paths cost 6 to 27 \% more instructions}: the second table for what the base does not have, and the test on every operand.}
\li{\strong{It does not show in time.} The level code is bound by memory, 2.26 instructions per cycle in rep-P11. In samples of the JVM launcher on cslib (8 workers, five passes, the efficiency cores of the host; one profile each) the frames of the level store, of its binding and of the machine's level cache are 9.0 \% of all samples before and 9.5 \% after. In the guard the workers' CPU outside the kernel falls by 1.5 \% on Mathlib and by 1.1 \% on cslib, which is the copies themselves; on the corpora with little to copy it is equal.}
\li{\strong{And it is not a gain either}: one shared base instead of eight copies in one shared cache was expected to take a part of the stalled cycles of the level code. Nothing of that size is visible. What is left for the level code is the layout of the records: a level's fields in five parallel arrays, a cache entry in three. That was not prototyped.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{7. What was not measured, and what was seen on the way}

\ul{
\li{\strong{The JVM launcher on a quiet host}: its guard was taken with the other cores busy only. The executable was measured in both conditions (sections 5.1 and 5.4).}
\li{\strong{x86-64}, the architecture of the arena; \strong{the optional profile-guided build}; \strong{one worker}; the wall clock of the reference engine and of the strict policy, which were gated and not timed.}
\li{\strong{The differential engine} of the test kit and the arena's own procedure were not run: the change computes nothing differently, and that claim rests on sections 3.1 to 3.4.}
\li{\strong{The invariance suite} on the mutants of the corpora \code{mdata} and \code{prelude}; with the reference engine on the corpus \code{bugs} and on the four large exports.}
\li{\strong{Level code under a profile of the executable.} The statement that the level code got no cheaper rests on one JVM profile per variant on cslib (efficiency cores, five passes each, 17,500 samples) and on the workers' CPU of the guard; no \code{perf} run of the image was made.}
\li{\strong{Seen on the way: two executables can differ by 5 \% on a unit that does not run the changed code.} The mutant of \code{bugs/proj-of-subst-prop} that changes a literal to 2^64 - 1 is one rejected declaration that grows the machine's value table to 3.7 GiB; a JVM profile of it has no frame of the level store, and it interns 361 level nodes in all. One worker, batch load, two alternating rounds in one window: 20.75 and 20.27 s in the base executable, 21.62 and 21.44 s in the candidate's; on the JVM launcher 13.47 and 13.81 s against 13.38 and 13.10 s. The level code is not what this unit executes, so the difference is in how the image was compiled or laid out around it. None of the eight corpora shows it (navier-stokes-euler, the corpus with the largest units, is within 0.3 \% in both conditions). It was not investigated further; it is a reason to read a difference of a few percent between two images on a single unit with care.}
\li{\strong{Seen on the way: the cache entries that survive a reset.} They must end at a rebase for the ids to be those of a new overlay (section 2.3). A build without that rule, run side by side with the old layout on Std, cedar and cslib, shows no differing id (14.8 M, 18.6 M and 96.1 M results): on these corpora the case does not occur, as the case of LV1-F1 does not.}
\li{\strong{Not done:} the loader's own copy per publication; a record layout for the level nodes and the cache entries; any change of the hand-off interval (section 5.3 only measures it).}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{8. For the integrator}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Patches}, against the tree of 2026-11-25 05:28 UTC, each verified with a dry run on the main tree: the layout (\src{rlvm-level/src/main/java/rlvm/level/LevelStore.java}, \src{rlvm-level/src/main/java/rlvm/level/IntListStore.java}, \src{rlvm-api/src/main/java/rlvm/api/bind/LevelBinding.java}, \src{rlvm-api/src/main/java/rlvm/api/bind/Bindings.java}); the tests (\src{rlvm-level/src/test/java/rlvm/level/OverlayTest.java}, the new \code{\verb~|SharedOverlayTest~}, \src{rlvm-api/src/test/java/rlvm/api/bind/BindingsTest.java}); the README of the module and the benchmark's switch.}
\li{\strong{Order of publication:} \code{rlvm-level} first, then \code{rlvm-api}. The old \code{rlvm-api} runs on the new \code{rlvm-level} unchanged (it then still copies); the new \code{rlvm-api} needs the new \code{rlvm-level}.}
\li{\strong{Interface.} Added: \code{\verb~|LevelStore.newSharedOverlay()~}, \code{\verb~|rebase(LevelStore)~}, \code{\verb~|isSharedOverlay()~}; \code{\verb~|IntListStore.poolOf(int)~}; \code{\verb~|LevelBinding.rebaseOnto(LevelStore)~}. Unchanged in meaning: \code{\verb~|newOverlay()~}, \code{\verb~|freeze()~}, \code{\verb~|reset()~}, the ids, \code{\verb~|LevelBinding.rebase(LevelStore)~}. Narrowed: \code{\verb~|IntListStore.pool()~} is an error on a shared overlay, which has two arrays; its readers outside the module are the two set operations of the binding, which now use \code{\verb~|poolOf~}, and the loader's level chain, whose open store is a flat one and is not affected. A shared overlay cannot be frozen. The machine's two reads of the base bounds (the check of a code block) see the same values as before.}
\li{\strong{The executable's profile} of the optional build was recorded with the old level code; it has to be recorded again with the release that takes this change.}
\li{\strong{Trees to revise when it is adopted:} the section "Frozen base and overlays" of [the design of universe levels](design-universe-levels), the paragraph on the hand-off interval in [the driver](design-driver), and a changelog entry of the area \code{reference-engine}.}
\li{\strong{Next in the same code}, not done here: the loader's one copy per publication; the hand-off interval, which was scaled up because of the workers' copies (section 5); records instead of parallel arrays for the level nodes and for the entries of the two instantiation caches.}
}
}
